WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.793
In two thousand six, Netflix released a hundred million movie ratings for a million-dollar prize.

00:00:05.793 --> 00:00:09.585
Names stripped, random I Ds in their place.

00:00:09.585 --> 00:00:13.378
Anonymous, they said.

00:00:13.378 --> 00:00:17.273
Start with the Netflix rows, anonymous, just random I Ds.

00:00:17.273 --> 00:00:21.169
The public IMDB reviews are separate, people posting under their real names.

00:00:21.169 --> 00:00:25.064
Each looks safe alone.

00:00:25.064 --> 00:00:28.959
So can the join really undo the anonymity?

00:00:28.959 --> 00:00:32.854
Yes.

00:00:32.854 --> 00:00:36.750
Match on the same movies, ratings, and dates, and the anonymous ID snaps to a real name.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.645
About eight rated movies make you ninety-nine percent unique.

00:00:40.645 --> 00:00:44.593
And it is not just movies.

00:00:44.593 --> 00:00:48.540
Date of birth, gender, and zip code identify eighty-seven percent of Americans.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.488
A handful of locations, or even login times, single you out.

00:00:52.488 --> 00:00:56.435
The attributes you leave behind are a fingerprint.

00:00:56.435 --> 00:01:00.614
So privacy is engineered with math.

00:01:00.614 --> 00:01:04.794
Differential privacy adds calibrated noise, so no single person changes the answer, while the totals stay accurate.

00:01:04.794 --> 00:01:08.974
A privacy budget, epsilon, tunes the noise.

00:01:08.974 --> 00:01:13.153
Lower epsilon, more privacy.

00:01:13.153 --> 00:01:16.787
So your data is a fingerprint, and anonymous is not private.

00:01:16.787 --> 00:01:20.421
Deleting names is not enough.

00:01:20.421 --> 00:01:24.055
Math is.

00:01:24.055 --> 00:01:27.689
To see it in the field, the case study covers how Google published COVID mobility reports without exposing anyone.
